body:
In four-vector notation, with \(x^\mu\coloneqq(ct,x,y,z)\), a Lorentz transformation is written \[ x'^\mu\overset{	ext{Lorentz}}{=}\Lambda^\mu{}_{
u}x^
u. \] The \(	ext{Lorentz}\) label marks the transformation law for four-vector components. Preservation of the metric is expressed by \[ \eta_{\alpha eta}\Lambda^\alpha{}_{\mu}\Lambda^ eta{}_{
u}\overset{	ext{metric preservation}}{=}\eta_{\mu
u}, \] or, in matrix notation, \[ \Lambda^T\eta\Lambda\overset{	ext{metric preservation}}{=}\eta. \] The \(	ext{metric preservation}\) label names the condition that makes \(\Lambda\) a Lorentz transformation.

In covariant form the Lorentz force law is \[  rac{dp^\mu}{d	au}\overset{	ext{Lorentz force}}{=}qF^\mu{}_{
u}U^
u, \] where \(p^\mu\) is the \cref{Momentum four-vector}{sr.momentum_four_vector}, \(U^
u\) is the \cref{Velocity four-vector}{sr.velocity_four_vector}, \(F^\mu{}_{
u}\) is the \cref{Field tensor}{sr.field_tensor}, and \(q\) is the particle's charge. The \(	ext{Lorentz force}\) label marks the dynamical law being imposed.

For fields \(\phi_a\) with Lagrangian density \(\mathcal L(\phi_a,\partial_\mu\phi_a)\), a canonical expression is \[ T^\mu{}_{
u}\overset{	ext{Noether}}{=} rac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi_a)}\partial_
u\phi_a-\delta^\mu{}_{
u}\mathcal L. \] The \(	ext{Noether}\) label marks that this canonical tensor comes from spacetime translation symmetry.
